# Guide: add a new model and query

Walks through adding a `widgets` table, following the same shape as
the existing `Item` model — copy that shape for any new model rather
than inventing a new one.

## 1. Define the model

In `src/{{ package_name }}/models.py`, add a class next to `Item`,
using the same `Mapped[...]`/`mapped_column(...)` style:

```python
class Widget(Base):
    __tablename__ = "widgets"

    id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(primary_key=True)
    name: Mapped[str]
    quantity: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(default=0)
```

`Mapped[str]` alone is a required column; `Mapped[str | None]` makes
it nullable; reach for `mapped_column(...)` when you need a default, a
`primary_key=True`, a `ForeignKey`, or an explicit column name.

{% if migrations %}
This project has `migrations=true` — this class change is only a
*definition*. It doesn't touch the actual database schema until you
generate and run a migration (see the `alembic` skill's
`guides/first-migration.md`) — do this before the routes below can
work against a real (non-test) database.
{% else %}
This project has `migrations=false` — `init_db()`'s `create_all()`
picks up the new table automatically on the next app start, no
migration step needed.
{% endif %}

## 2. Add request/response schemas

In `src/{{ package_name }}/schemas.py`, mirroring `ItemCreate`/`ItemRead`:

```python
class WidgetCreate(BaseModel):
    name: str
    quantity: int = 0


class WidgetRead(BaseModel):
    id: int
    name: str
    quantity: int

    model_config = {"from_attributes": True}
```

## 3. Write the queries

{% if framework == "fastapi" %}
Create `src/{{ package_name }}/routes/widgets.py`, following
`routes/items.py`'s shape exactly:

```python
from fastapi import APIRouter, Depends, HTTPException
from sqlalchemy import select
from sqlalchemy.ext.asyncio import AsyncSession

from {{ package_name }}.core.db import get_session
from {{ package_name }}.models import Widget
from {{ package_name }}.schemas import WidgetCreate, WidgetRead

router = APIRouter(prefix="/widgets", tags=["widgets"])


@router.get("/", response_model=list[WidgetRead])
async def list_widgets(session: AsyncSession = Depends(get_session)) -> list[Widget]:
    result = await session.execute(select(Widget))
    return list(result.scalars().all())


@router.post("/", response_model=WidgetRead, status_code=201)
async def create_widget(widget: WidgetCreate, session: AsyncSession = Depends(get_session)) -> Widget:
    db_widget = Widget(**widget.model_dump())
    session.add(db_widget)
    await session.commit()
    await session.refresh(db_widget)
    return db_widget


@router.get("/{widget_id}", response_model=WidgetRead)
async def get_widget(widget_id: int, session: AsyncSession = Depends(get_session)) -> Widget:
    widget = await session.get(Widget, widget_id)
    if widget is None:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail="Widget not found")
    return widget


@router.delete("/{widget_id}", status_code=204)
async def delete_widget(widget_id: int, session: AsyncSession = Depends(get_session)) -> None:
    widget = await session.get(Widget, widget_id)
    if widget is None:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail="Widget not found")
    await session.delete(widget)
    await session.commit()
```

Every session call here is a coroutine except `session.add(...)` —
missing an `await` on `execute`/`get`/`commit`/`refresh`/`delete`
returns a coroutine object instead of the result, which surfaces as a
confusing type error, not a silent bug.

## 4. Register the router

In `src/{{ package_name }}/main.py`:

```python
from {{ package_name }}.routes.widgets import router as widgets_router
...
app.include_router(widgets_router)
```

## 5. Test it

Add `tests/test_widgets.py`, mirroring `tests/test_main.py`'s `client`
fixture (async, hits the app via `ASGITransport` — see the `fastapi`
skill's `guides/testing.md`) — one CRUD-cycle test (create, list, get,
delete, and the 404 case) rather than one large test asserting
everything.
{% else %}
Create `src/{{ package_name }}/routes/widgets.py`, following
`routes/items.py`'s shape exactly:

```python
from flask import Blueprint, jsonify, request
from pydantic import ValidationError
from sqlalchemy import select

from {{ package_name }}.core.db import get_session
from {{ package_name }}.models import Widget
from {{ package_name }}.schemas import WidgetCreate, WidgetRead

bp = Blueprint("widgets", __name__, url_prefix="/widgets")


@bp.get("/")
def list_widgets():
    session = get_session()
    widgets = session.execute(select(Widget)).scalars().all()
    return jsonify([WidgetRead.model_validate(w).model_dump() for w in widgets])


@bp.post("/")
def create_widget():
    try:
        payload = WidgetCreate.model_validate(request.get_json(force=True, silent=True) or {})
    except ValidationError as exc:
        return jsonify({"detail": exc.errors()}), 422
    session = get_session()
    widget = Widget(**payload.model_dump())
    session.add(widget)
    session.commit()
    return jsonify(WidgetRead.model_validate(widget).model_dump()), 201


@bp.get("/<int:widget_id>")
def get_widget(widget_id: int):
    session = get_session()
    widget = session.get(Widget, widget_id)
    if widget is None:
        return jsonify({"detail": "Widget not found"}), 404
    return jsonify(WidgetRead.model_validate(widget).model_dump())


@bp.delete("/<int:widget_id>")
def delete_widget(widget_id: int):
    session = get_session()
    widget = session.get(Widget, widget_id)
    if widget is None:
        return jsonify({"detail": "Widget not found"}), 404
    session.delete(widget)
    session.commit()
    return "", 204
```

Nothing here is awaited — this is plain sync SQLAlchemy, matching
Flask's sync handlers. `get_session()` returns the same thread-local
session for the whole request (see `references/sessions-and-queries.md`),
so calling it again in each handler is cheap and normal, not wasteful.

## 4. Register the blueprint

In `src/{{ package_name }}/main.py`:

```python
from {{ package_name }}.routes.widgets import bp as widgets_bp
...
app.register_blueprint(widgets_bp)
```

## 5. Test it

Add `tests/test_widgets.py`, mirroring `tests/test_main.py`'s `client`
fixture (`create_app(database_url="sqlite:///:memory:", testing=True)`,
see the `flask` skill) — one CRUD-cycle test (create, list, get,
delete, and the 404 case) rather than one large test asserting
everything.
{% endif %}
